'''arrestment''' (a̤-rest′ment), ''n.'' [< OF. ''arestement'', < ''arester'', arrest: see ''arrest''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.'', and ''-ment''.] '''1.''' The act of arresting or stopping; obstruction; stoppage.
{{smaller block|The first effect is ''arrestment'' of the functions of the spinal cord. {{right|''Sir R. Christison'', Poisons, I. i. § 2.}}
The fall of man would produce an ''arrestment'' in the progress of the earth in that last great revolution which would have converted it into an Eden.
{{right|''Dawson'', Origin of World, p. 239.}}}}
'''2.''' In ''Scots law'': (''a'') A process by which a creditor may attach money or movable property which a third person holds for behoof of his debtor. It bears a general resemblance to foreign attachment by the custom of London. See ''attachment''. (''b'') The arrest or detention of a criminal till he finds caution or surety to stand trial, or the securing of a debtor until he pays the debt or gives security for its payment.—'''Breach of arrestment.''' {{smaller|See ''breach''.}}

